d be for a different  reason but the optics and also just the  the overall logistics were you know  greatly concerned a lot of people the  second sort of concern about the death  penalty was that this was really an  obstacle for transferring cases from the  tribunal and from foreign jurisdictions  the rules of procedure and evidence of  the Rwanda tribunal itself dictates that  cases cannot be transferred to any  jurisdictions that implement the death  penalty so the part of the legacy is  that the Rwandan government decided to  abolish the death penalty in o7 by that  time it had actually been several years  since and he definitely actually been  even carried out and in some ways this  really contributed to the overall  campaign the global campaign to abolish  the death penalty for third party states  for foreign countries there are a number  of legacies as well one is one concerns  universal jurisdiction which I mentioned  before even though it's it's a very  controversial claim legally as well as  politically some aspire to this idea  that that really when it comes to  genocide and other atrocity crimes these  these should concern us so much that  that there they really ought to be dealt  with anywhere this has been implemented  to varying degrees in different  countries and has also received a lot of  pushback for various reasons there's  also been incorporation of atrocity laws  enabling legislation in various domestic  and national jurisdictions that allows  domestic polities domestic countries  jurisdictions courts to prosecute for  atrocities and then another legacy is  that  as we know the the new now permanence  war crimes tribunal the International  Criminal Court with potentially  unlimited jurisdiction globally has been  signed by over a hundred countries all  over the world there have been other  legacies also from internationally from  the International Criminal Tribunal for  Rwanda itself so three just to point out  because there's such precedent set