unals but in the case of the ice-t  our this was the very first time that's  a tribunal had been created for a purely  civil conflict establishing the  precedent for even internal crimes which  relates to I think the RTP the r2p  concept it's now led also to the  proliferation of tribunals in in various  countries around the world and for  various scenarios Sierra Leone Cambodia  Iraq Lebanon and also the creation of  the the new permanent International  Criminal Court one of the things I  should point out is in in my research  this is been very evident that there's a  safe an amount of path dependence which  relates to the design and whether or not  to even create a transitional justice  mechanism in the first place the ICR  from what I can conclude was largely  created because the ICTY had recently  been created and as we look at how  weather and then how tribunals and other  transitional justice mechanisms are  created around the world it is vital to  look at what already exists and what  comes before it  in many ways they're used exactly as as  models for what comes later  some of the individual legacies that  derive from the ice-t are include that  the prosecutor there was a shared  prosecutor of the ice-t UI and the ice-t  are initially and basically the reason  was because it would been so difficult  to find a suitable candidate in the case  of the Sui that person in the form of  justice Goldstone from South Africa was  then just sort of had an expanded  mandate to include Rwanda but the  problem was that the the people who had  served in his and who later served in  his capacity in his position as chief  speeder of the shared tribunals spent  very little time focused on on Rwanda  some estimates are that in some years as  little as thirty five days were spent  actually in Arusha at the other tribe  you know this led to lots of management  problems problems and so the legacies  from that were that were included that  the the tribunal itself separated the  tri